---
/**
 * Portfolio two: six funds that lean a third of the money toward cheap, small companies.
 * The verdict is "probably", and the page carries both the gap over a plain index and
 * the seventeen-year stretch cheap stocks spent behind the market. Every number that
 * describes what the portfolio did is read from the series the research emitter wrote.
 */
import Callout from "~/components/Callout.astro";
import Donut from "~/components/Donut.astro";
import DrawdownChart from "~/components/DrawdownChart.astro";
import Figure from "~/components/Figure.astro";
import GrowthChart from "~/components/GrowthChart.astro";
import Hero from "~/components/Hero.astro";
import StatGrid from "~/components/StatGrid.astro";
import Verdict from "~/components/Verdict.astro";
import { portfolio } from "~/content/portfolios";
import { formatDollars, formatMonths, formatPct } from "~/lib/charts";
import { fundColour } from "~/lib/palette";
import { seriesFor } from "~/lib/series";
import Base from "~/layouts/Base.astro";

const p = portfolio("value-lean");

const valueLean = seriesFor("value-lean");
const market = seriesFor("market");

const s = valueLean.summary;
const fall = s.worstFall;
const m = market.summary;

/** "2007-10" as "October 2007". */
function monthName(date: string): string {
  const [year, month] = date.split("-").map(Number);
  return new Date(year ?? 1970, (month ?? 1) - 1, 1).toLocaleString("en-US", { month: "long", year: "numeric" });
}

const dollars = (value: number) => formatDollars(value, "label");
const pct = (value: number, decimals = 1) => formatPct(value, decimals);
const year = (key: string) => s.calendarYears[key] ?? Number.NaN;

const STATS = [
  { value: dollars(s.final), label: "$10,000 became", note: `Against ${dollars(m.final)} for a plain world stock index` },
  { value: pct(s.cagrPct), label: "Grew a year", note: `Against ${pct(m.cagrPct)} for the index` },
  {
    value: pct(fall.pct),
    unit: dollars(fall.dollarsAtTrough),
    label: "Worst fall",
    note: `${monthName(fall.peak)} to ${monthName(fall.trough)}; back to even in ${formatMonths(fall.monthsToRecover)}`,
  },
  { value: p.feeOn10k, label: "Costs a year on $10,000", note: `${p.fee} fee` },
];

const COLUMNS = ["Fund", "What it holds", "Share", "Fee on $10,000", "Account"] as const;
const HOLDINGS = [
  {
    ticker: "VTI",
    name: "Vanguard Morningstar Total Stock Market ETF",
    holds: "The whole US stock market",
    weight: 49,
    fee: "$3",
    account: "Taxable account",
  },
  {
    ticker: "VXUS",
    name: "Vanguard Total International Stock ETF",
    holds: "Every stock market outside the US",
    weight: 16,
    fee: "$5",
    account: "Retirement account",
  },
  {
    ticker: "VTV",
    name: "Vanguard Morningstar Value ETF",
    holds: "Large US companies priced cheaply",
    weight: 15,
    fee: "$3",
    account: "Either",
  },
  {
    ticker: "AVDV",
    name: "Avantis International Small Cap Value ETF",
    holds: "Small, cheap, profitable foreign companies",
    weight: 10,
    fee: "$36",
    account: "Retirement account, Roth first",
  },
  {
    ticker: "IDMO",
    name: "Invesco S&P International Developed Momentum ETF",
    holds: "Foreign companies whose shares are rising",
    weight: 5,
    fee: "$25",
    account: "Retirement account",
  },
  {
    ticker: "AVES",
    name: "Avantis Emerging Markets Value ETF",
    holds: "Cheap companies in emerging markets",
    weight: 5,
    fee: "$36",
    account: "Retirement account",
  },
];
---

<Base
  title="A lean toward value"
  xl
  description="Six funds that lean a third of the money toward cheaper, smaller companies. What $10,000 did since 1990, the seventeen years cheap stocks spent behind the market, what the lean added over a plain index, and which fund to drop first."
>
  <Hero
    eyebrow="Portfolio 2 of 4"
    title="A lean toward value"
    lede="The whole market, with a third of the money leaned toward companies that are cheap and small. Since 1990 the lean has added about 0.8% a year over a plain index, and it has trailed the market for seventeen years at a stretch."
  >
    <p class="mt-4 flex flex-wrap items-baseline gap-x-3 gap-y-1 text-ink-2">
      <Verdict word="Probably" /> <span>Ahead on every stretch tested, on two histories; thirty years would be needed to rule out luck.</span>
    </p>
  </Hero>

  <div class="prose w-full mt-10">
    <h2>What you buy</h2>
  </div>

  <div class="wide mt-4 grid items-start gap-6 md:grid-cols-[minmax(0,1fr)_12rem]">
    <table role="table" class="stack-table w-full border-collapse text-base">
      <caption class="sr-only">The six funds, what each holds, its share, its fee on $10,000 and which account to hold it in</caption>
      <thead role="rowgroup">
        <tr role="row" class="border-b border-rule-strong text-left align-bottom">
          {COLUMNS.map((c) => <th role="columnheader" scope="col" class="label py-2 pr-3 font-semibold">{c}</th>)}
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody role="rowgroup" class="text-ink-2">
        {
          HOLDINGS.map((h) => (
            <tr role="row" class="border-b border-rule align-top last:border-0">
              <th role="rowheader" scope="row" class="py-2 pr-3 text-left font-medium text-ink">
                <span class="inline-flex items-baseline gap-2">
                  <span class="donut-swatch" style={`background:${fundColour(h.ticker)}`} aria-hidden="true" />
                  <span data-numeric>{h.ticker}</span>
                </span>
                <span class="block text-sm font-normal text-ink-2">{h.name}</span>
              </th>
              <td role="cell" data-label={COLUMNS[1]} class="py-2 pr-3">{h.holds}</td>
              <td role="cell" data-label={COLUMNS[2]} data-numeric class="py-2 pr-3">{h.weight}%</td>
              <td role="cell" data-label={COLUMNS[3]} data-numeric class="py-2 pr-3">{h.fee}</td>
              <td role="cell" data-label={COLUMNS[4]} class="py-2">{h.account}</td>
            </tr>
          ))
        }
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <Donut
      holdings={HOLDINGS.map((h) => ({ ticker: h.ticker, weight: h.weight, label: h.holds }))}
      center={{ value: p.fee, label: "a year" }}
      legend={false}
      name="Value lean"
      class="justify-center md:justify-end"
    />
  </div>

  <div class="prose w-full mt-6">
    <p>Two thirds of the money, VTI and VXUS, is the plain market. The other third is the lean: VTV for large cheap
    US companies, AVDV for small cheap foreign ones, AVES for cheap companies in emerging markets, and IDMO for foreign
    shares that have been rising, a different idea from cheapness. AVES is under five years old.</p>

    <p>VTV is the working choice for US value. The comparison with AVUV, which buys smaller companies, remains
    inconclusive; the evidence leaves room for a better fund or a different allocation.</p>

    <p>For fewer funds, you can move IDMO or AVES into VXUS. Removing IDMO gives up the bet on rising shares;
    removing AVES gives up the extra lean toward cheap emerging-market companies. Both simplify the portfolio,
    but neither change guarantees a better return. Frequent trading inside an ETF does not by itself tell you
    how much taxable income it will pay out.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="prose w-full mt-14">
    <h2>What $10,000 did</h2>
  </div>

  <StatGrid items={STATS} class="wide mt-4" />

  <GrowthChart series={[valueLean, market]} highlight="value-lean" class="wide mt-8" title="What $10,000 became">
    The value lean against a plain world stock index (a 65/35 mix of US and international index funds, which is also
    <a href="/portfolios/one-fund/">portfolio 1</a>), 1990 to 2026. Simulated from index data, not fund records: each
    fund is stood in for by the indexes it tracks, less its fee and a trading charge.
  </GrowthChart>

  <div class="prose w-full mt-6">
    <p>Best calendar year: {s.bestYear?.year}, up {pct(s.bestYear?.pct ?? Number.NaN)}. Worst: {s.worstYear?.year},
    down {pct(Math.abs(s.worstYear?.pct ?? Number.NaN))}. In 2020 it made {pct(year("2020"))}; in 2022 it lost
    {pct(Math.abs(year("2022")))}.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="prose w-full mt-14">
    <h2>The worst fall</h2>
  </div>

  <DrawdownChart series={[valueLean, market]} highlight="value-lean" worst={fall} class="wide mt-4" title="Falls from the last high">
    How far below its last high $10,000 sat, month by month, against a plain world stock index. Zero is the last
    high; every dip is a fall from it.
  </DrawdownChart>

  <div class="prose w-full mt-6">
    <p>It was back above $10,000 in {monthName(fall.recovered ?? fall.trough)}, {formatMonths(fall.monthsToRecover)}
    after the {monthName(fall.peak)} peak. The plain index fell to {dollars(m.worstFall.dollarsAtTrough)} and took
    {formatMonths(m.worstFall.monthsToRecover)}. The lean falls with the market; the hard part is the quiet stretch.
    Cheap US stocks ran <Figure id="value-drawdown" inline /> from September 2008, and have not caught up. Foreign
    stocks ran <Figure id="intl-drawdown" inline />, measured against US stocks.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="prose w-full mt-14">
    <h2>What it beat, and by how much</h2>

    <p>Against a plain index mix with the same split of US and foreign stocks, these six funds earned about 0.8% a
    year more over 1990 to 2026, after fees and trading costs, with a plausible range of 0.4% to 1.3%. The gap was
    positive in all seven stretches the data was cut into. On $10,000 that is about {dollars(s.final - m.final)}
    more.</p>

    <p>Because the funds were chosen with that data in view, I ran the same lean on July 1981 to October 1990. It
    earned about 0.9% a year more, range 0.4% to 1.3%, positive in both halves of the decade. That confirms the
    direction, not the size: on that data source's basis the 1990 to 2026 gap reads only 0.4%.</p>

    <p>Since 1994, after the idea was published, cheap stocks beat expensive ones by about 4.7% a year across the
    US, other rich countries and emerging markets together, on paper and before fees, range 1.5% to 8.1%. Almost all
    of it came from outside the US: the US alone gave 1.6% a year, too small to tell from luck, and 79% of that
    arrived in the single year 2000.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="prose w-full mt-14">
    <h2>Who it is for</h2>

    <p>Someone who can watch a plain US index fund pull ahead for fifteen years without selling. A shortfall of 45%
    over eighteen years is inside what this portfolio has done and is not evidence anything is broken.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="prose w-full mt-14">
    <h2>How to hold it</h2>
  </div>

  <div class="measure">
    <Callout kind="do-this" class="mt-4">
      <p>Buy the six funds in the shares above. If you have a retirement account and a taxable account, the foreign
      funds and IDMO go in the retirement account, VTV and VTI in the taxable account. Add money when you have it, to
      whatever is furthest below its target. Rebalance once a year with new money, and never sell in the taxable
      account to do it. Do nothing when it falls. The details are on <a href="/how-to-hold/">how to hold it</a>.</p>
    </Callout>
  </div>

  <div class="prose w-full mt-14">
    <h2>What could go wrong</h2>

    <p>The lean can lose for longer than you can wait, and the stretch arrives with no warning. In
    the worst tenth of stock months the three cheap-stock funds move together far more than usual, so five ideas
    shrink to about three. And a fund that only buys cheap stocks, and cannot bet against expensive ones, captures
    about half of the paper gap by arithmetic.</p>

    <p>IDMO is the fund most likely to disappoint. Buying what has been rising beat the market on paper by about 7.3%
    a year since 1994, but a paper version's trading cost, 3.3% to 18.7% a year, is as wide as the
    whole gain, and all three regions crashed together in 2009: US momentum fell 52.9%, developed foreign 36.8%,
    emerging 28.9%. A US momentum fund such as SPMO shares some of IDMO's risks. Whether holding both improves
    the portfolio remains unresolved; overlap alone does not settle it.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="prose w-full mt-14">
    <h2>The long history</h2>

    <p>This portfolio has no 1929 history: the foreign data it rests on begins in 1990. The nearest thing is the 1981
    to 1990 check above.</p>
  </div>
</Base>
